Yes. You already receive the displayed Mapillary image ID here:

```js
.then((randomLocationImageId) => {
  viewer.moveTo(randomLocationImageId);
});
```

Store it in shared frontend state. Since regular `<script>` files share the global scope, define the variable outside the event listener in `streetview.js`:

```js
// streetview.js
let currentImageId = null;

document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", async () => {
  const streetviewContainer = document.querySelector("#streetview-container");

  const tokenResponse = await fetch("/streetview/access-token");
  const { accessToken } = await tokenResponse.json();

  const viewer = new mapillary.Viewer({
    accessToken,
    container: streetviewContainer,
  });

  fetch("/streetview/random-location")
    .then((res) => res.json())
    .then((randomLocationImageId) => {
      currentImageId = String(randomLocationImageId);

      return viewer.moveTo(currentImageId);
    })
    .catch((error) => {
      console.error("Unable to load Mapillary image", error);
      streetviewContainer.textContent = "Unable to load streetview image";
    });
});
```

Then use that variable in `guess_map.js`:

```js
aiReviewButton.addEventListener("click", async () => {
  if (!currentImageId) {
    console.error("Street View image has not loaded yet.");
    return;
  }

  const response = await fetch("/streetview/ai-review", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      imageId: currentImageId,
    }),
  });

  const aiReview = await response.json();

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(aiReview.error || "AI review request failed");
  }

  console.log(aiReview.review);
});
```

This works because `guess_map.js` and `streetview.js` are both regular scripts in the same page. The script order does not cause a problem because the click handler only reads `currentImageId` after the user clicks.

A slightly safer alternative is to use `window.currentImageId` explicitly:

```js
window.currentImageId = String(randomLocationImageId);
```

and then:

```js
body: JSON.stringify({
  imageId: window.currentImageId,
});
```

The important detail is to keep the ID as a string, since large Mapillary IDs can lose precision if treated as JavaScript numbers.

> question 1: What if I have two users playing the game separately on their browser at the same time? Will this still work, since each browser has its own running instance of the frontend code?
>
> question 2: Is this clean code? Can the user maliciously put bad imageIds in the request made to the backend?

1. Yes. Each browser tab has its own JavaScript runtime and its own `currentImageId` variable. Two users—or two tabs—will not overwrite each other’s frontend state.

The backend request is also independent because the ID is sent with each individual request:

```js
body: JSON.stringify({ imageId: currentImageId });
```

The main exception would be if you stored the image ID in a shared backend/global variable. Avoid doing that. Keep it in the browser or associate it with a user session.

2. The frontend approach is clean enough, but the backend must treat `imageId` as untrusted input. A malicious user can manually send:

```js
fetch("/streetview/ai-review", {
  method: "POST",
  body: JSON.stringify({ imageId: "some-other-id" }),
});
```

Your backend already does an important check:

```js
if (!/^\d+$/.test(imageId)) {
  return res.status(400).json(...);
}
```

That prevents malformed IDs, but it does not prove that the ID belongs to the image assigned to that user’s current round. If that matters, validate it server-side:

- Store the selected image ID in the user’s session or database.
- When `/random-location` returns an image, associate it with that session/round.
- In `/ai-review`, compare the submitted ID against the stored ID.
- Reject the request if they do not match.

For example:

```js
if (String(req.session.imageId) !== imageId) {
  return res.status(403).json({
    error: "Image does not belong to the current round",
  });
}
```

Also consider rate limiting the AI-review endpoint, since users could repeatedly call it and consume your Gemini/Mapillary API quota.

So: frontend state is isolated per user, but frontend state is never a security boundary. The backend should validate ownership, format, authorization, and request frequency.
